  pEn=[
   "In the year 950 the chronicle of the Welsh, the Annales Cambriae, set down a single line: Hywel, king of the Britons, has died. He had ruled almost the whole of Wales, from Ynys Môn to the coast of Dyfed, and he had kept his people at peace in a hard century. Wales gave him a name it gave to no other king: Hywel Dda, Hywel the Good. A thousand years later that name is still joined to the law that held the nation together for six centuries, Cyfraith Hywel, the Law of Hywel.",
   "> Hywel, king of the Britons, has died. | Annales Cambriae, under the year 950",
   "|| Born | about 880",
   "|| Grandfather | Rhodri Mawr, king of Gwynedd",
   "|| Wife | Elen, daughter of Llywarch ap Hyfaidd, king of Dyfed",
   "|| His kingdoms | Seisyllwg and Dyfed, joined as Deheubarth; then Gwynedd and Powys from 942",
   "|| Journey to Rome | 928",
   "|| The gathering at Whitland | about 945, by tradition",
   "|| Died | 950",
   "|| His law was used until | the Acts of Union, 1536 and 1543",
   "## The grandson of Rhodri Mawr",
   "Hywel ap Cadell was born about 880, a grandson of Rhodri Mawr, the king who had beaten the Vikings on Ynys Môn. When Rhodri’s lands passed to his sons, Hywel’s father Cadell received Seisyllwg, the country of Ceredigion and Ystrad Tywi. Hywel married Elen, daughter of Llywarch ap Hyfaidd, king of Dyfed, and through that marriage the old kingdom of the far south-west came to him.",
   "After his brother Clydog died in 920, Hywel joined Dyfed and Seisyllwg into one kingdom. It took the name Deheubarth, ‘the southern part’, and it was the great kingdom of the south for three hundred years, the kingdom of the Lord Rhys and of Dinefwr. Then in 942 Idwal Foel, king of Gwynedd, fell fighting the English, and Hywel took Gwynedd and Powys as well. For the last eight years of his life he ruled every part of Wales except Morgannwg and Gwent in the south-east.",
   "## A king who chose peace",
   "Hywel looked beyond Wales. In 928 he made the long pilgrimage to Rome, a journey the Welsh annals thought worth recording beside the deaths of kings. He knew the court of the English king Æthelstan well, and dealt with the most powerful ruler in Britain face to face; his name stands among the witnesses to Æthelstan’s charters.",
   "Some of the poets of his day called for war. Armes Prydein, The Prophecy of Britain, foretold that the Cymry would rise with the Irish, the Norse of Dublin and the Britons of Cornwall and Strathclyde, and drive the English out of the island. Hywel chose another road. He kept the peace, and with it he kept his lands whole and his people safe while the rest of Britain was at war.",
   "He is also the only Welsh king known to have struck his own coin. A single silver penny survives, made for him at Chester by a moneyer named Gillys, with his name and the title rex, king, around the edge. It is kept in the British Museum.",
   "## Six men from every part of Wales",
   "The lawbooks themselves tell how their law began. Hywel, they say, saw that the customs of Wales had become tangled and were often broken. So he called to Tŷ Gwyn ar Daf, the White House on the Taf, at Whitland in what is now Carmarthenshire, six men from every part of the land, together with the bishops, abbots and learned clergy of Wales. They gathered in Lent, and spent the forty days in prayer and fasting, asking for God’s help to make good law.",
   "From them Hywel chose the twelve wisest laymen, and one scholar, Blegywryd, a master of law, to write it down. They kept the old law where it was good, mended it where it was not, and made new law where it was needed. When it was done the law was read before the whole gathering, and the bishops laid the ban of the church on anyone who would break it. Three copies were made, the books say: one for the court that travelled with the king, one for Aberffraw in the north and one for Dinefwr in the south. And Hywel went to Rome with his bishops to have the law read before the Pope, and came home with its blessing.",
   "The copies we have were written in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries, and the law grew and changed as lawyers used it, so scholars still study which parts go back to Hywel’s own day. But every lawyer of medieval Wales, in the north and in the south, gave the law the same name. It was Cyfraith Hywel, and it was the law of the whole nation.",
   "## The king’s court",
   "The first part of the law describes the king’s court, and through it we can step into a Welsh royal hall a thousand years ago. Twenty-four officers served the king, each with his own rights, his own place in the hall and his own share of the gifts. There was the penteulu, captain of the household warband; the chaplain; the steward; the court judge; the falconer; the chief groom; the physician, who treated the household without charge; and the footholder, who sat at the feast holding the king’s feet in his lap until the king went to bed. The queen had officers of her own.",
   "The bard of the household had his place too. When the warband went into battle, the law says, he was to sing to them Unbennaeth Prydain, The Sovereignty of Britain. Centuries after the legions left, the court of Wales still sang of the whole island as the inheritance of the Britons.",
   "Honour was measured with care. The sarhaed of the king, the payment owed for an insult to him, was a hundred cows for every cantref he ruled, and, as the books describe it, a rod of silver as tall as the king and a plate of gold as broad as his face.",
   "## Peace instead of revenge",
   "At the heart of the law was a simple idea: a wrong should be put right, not paid back in blood. If one man killed another, the killer’s kindred, out to distant cousins, paid galanas, a blood-price, to the kindred of the dead. Each cousin paid his share and each cousin of the victim received his. A killing became a debt that two families settled together, instead of a feud that could run for generations.",
   "An injury or an insult was answered by sarhaed, a payment for the shame, set by the standing of the person wronged. Every wound had its value in the law. A man accused could clear himself on oath, backed by the sworn word of his neighbours, and the more serious the charge, the more oath-helpers he needed. Bargains were kept by a mach, a surety, who promised before witnesses to see the debt paid.",
   "## The worth of everything",
   "The law set a value on everything a household might own, so that any loss could be paid for fairly. Even the cat had its price, and it rose as the cat proved itself:",
   "|| A kitten, from birth until its eyes open | 1 penny",
   "|| A kitten, until it has caught a mouse | 2 pence",
   "|| A cat that has caught mice | 4 pence",
   "|| The harp of the king | 120 pence",
   "A cat had to be able to see, to hear, to catch mice and to rear its kittens, and a buyer who found it could not was owed part of his money back. The cat that guarded the king’s barn was worth more again. In one version of the law, if anyone killed it, the cat was held up by its tail with its nose to the floor, and the killer poured wheat over it until the tip of its tail was covered. That heap of grain was the price.",
   "## Women and the law",
   "Women in Hywel’s Wales had rights that women in much of Europe would wait centuries for. A bride brought her gwaddol, her portion from her family. On the morning after the wedding her husband gave her the cowyll, a gift that was hers alone. If the marriage ended within seven years she left with her agweddi, a sum set by her rank.",
   "After seven years a marriage was a partnership of equals in property. If the couple parted, everything they owned was divided in two, and the law set out exactly how: the pigs to the husband and the sheep to the wife, and so on through the cattle, the household goods and the bedding. If a husband was unfaithful, the law paid his wife compensation, and if he did it again she could leave with everything that was hers.",
   "## Children and land",
   "Land was shared fairly too. When a father died his land was divided equally among all his sons, not handed to the eldest alone, and the youngest son kept the family home. A son born outside marriage had the same right as his brothers once his father acknowledged him. In Welsh law a child was not made to pay for the way he came into the world.",
   "## Bread for the hungry",
   "One rule is remembered above all the others. A person in need who had walked through three townships, and called at nine houses in each without being given food or shelter, and who then took food to stay alive, was not to be punished as a thief. A thousand years ago, the law of Wales would not make hunger a crime.",
   "## The books that survive",
   "Scholars group the Welsh lawbooks in three families. Llyfr Cyfnerth comes from the border country between the Wye and the Severn. Llyfr Blegywryd, which carries the name of Hywel’s scholar, is the law of Deheubarth. Llyfr Iorwerth is the lawbook of Gwynedd, revised by the lawyer Iorwerth ap Madog in the days of Llywelyn Fawr. There are Latin versions as well, made for the clergy and for courts that worked in Latin.",
   "Some forty manuscripts of the law survive. The Black Book of Chirk, written about 1250, is the oldest copy of the law in Welsh and one of the oldest books of Welsh prose in existence. Peniarth 28, a Latin copy of about the same age, is decorated with little pictures of the people of the law: the king on his throne, the judge, the falconer with his hawk, the physician, and the animals whose worth the law set down. Both are kept in the National Library of Wales at Aberystwyth, and you can turn their pages online.",
   "## Six hundred years",
   "The law was part of what made the Welsh a nation, and the princes defended it. When Edward I tried to have a dispute over the land of Arwystli tried under English law, Llywelyn ap Gruffudd answered that every land under the king’s rule, Gascony and Scotland among them, kept its own laws and customs, and that Wales should keep hers. The right to Welsh law was one of the causes he fought for in his last war.",
   "After 1282 the Statute of Rhuddlan brought English criminal law into the lands of the princes, but even then Cyfraith Hywel lived on. Welsh rules for land, family and contracts were still used across the country, and in the March Welsh courts sat into the sixteenth century. Only the Acts of Union of 1536 and 1543 brought the law to an end, some six hundred years after the gathering on the Taf.",
   "## Hywel today",
   "At Whitland the Hywel Dda Centre and its memorial garden stand where tradition places the gathering, the garden laid out in parts that each tell of one side of the law. The health board of west Wales carries his name: Hywel Dda University Health Board. And in 2012 the National Assembly passed its first Act, a law made in Wales for Wales, in Welsh and English with equal standing. The Senedd has been making Welsh law ever since.",
   "A thousand years after his death, Hywel’s name still stands for what he gave Wales: a law that made peace between families, set a fair price on every loss, treated a wife as a partner, and would not let the hungry be punished for being hungry. That is why Wales calls him the Good."],
